__notes__.note.rungs.setting-exact-part-what-tower:
\begin{quote}
\emph{Hypothesis (R), the rung reading.} The internal state space of an $n$-twistor composite is the spinor module of the $n$-th rung of the tower above $\Cl(2,4)$, $\Lambda^{\bullet}(W \oplus R_{n})$, on which the transported algebra acts as in Fact \claimref{rungs.imported-tower-one-qubit-per-rung}: each twistor of the composite occupies one new rung, and the constituents are the states with one rung occupied.
\end{quote}

lepton.relative-handedness-slotted-free-.formal:
Assume (C), and list the doublets $Q = \Con_{1}$ and $L = \Con_{3}$ as particles of one handedness. (The arithmetic is that of \cite[Thm.~5.5(3), last clause]{ButlerT12}, with the signs of the two nonzero sums reversed, since T12 conjugates the doublets and this note conjugates the free sector, so the lists are overall conjugates; what is new is only its reading under (C).) There are two ways to list the free sector $\Sp = \Lambda^{\bullet}W$ with them.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If the $\CS$ half of the free sector (grades $0$ and $2$) is listed with the $\CSm{}$ half of the slot sector, where $\CS = \Lambda^{\mathrm{even}}W$ is the conformal spinor space and $\CSm{} = \Lambda^{\mathrm{odd}}W$ its partner (twistor and dual twistor space), the two chirality halves of $\Sp$, the list is $Q, L, (1)_{0}, (\bar{3})_{-2/3}$, and the conjugates of the free $\CSm{}$ half, $(\bar{3})_{+1/3}$ and $(1)_{+1}$: that is $Q, L, \nu^{c}, u^{c}, d^{c}, e^{c}$, one Standard Model generation with a right-handed neutrino, and all four anomaly conditions vanish.
\item If the $\CSm{}$ half of the free sector is listed with the slot sector's, the list is $Q, L, (3)_{-1/3}, (1)_{-1}$ and the conjugates $(1)_{0}, (3)_{+2/3}$, and the $[SU(3)]^{2}U(1)$ and cubic conditions read $+\tfrac23$ and $-\tfrac49$.
\end{enumerate}
So the consistency of gauging fixes the relative handedness, the bit $\epsilon$ of Hypothesis (C): the chirality half of a free conformal spinor that is listed with the constituents' $\CSm{}$ is $\CS$, that is, $\epsilon = -1$, a slot reverses the half. Equivalently, in physical terms, the free-sector fields are $\nu^{c}$ and $u^{c}$ from $\CS$ and $d_{R}$ and $e_{R}$ from $\CSm{}$, four Weyl fields of the observed charges with no conjugation applied to any of them, and a bare free conformal spinor is the neutrino singlet while a bare conformal spinor in a slot is the antilepton.

lepton.unit-action-cell-action.formal:
Let $R_{k} \subset \Lambda^{k}(\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3})$ be the span of the single-row $k$-pair states $\bigwedge_{a \in S}(e_{i} \otimes f_{a})$, $i \in \{0, 1\}$, $S \subset \{1,2,3\}$ with $|S| = k$, and identify $R_{k}$ with $\Con_{k}$ by $\bigwedge_{a \in S}(e_{i} \otimes f_{a}) \mapsto e_{i} \otimes f_{S}$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item For $k = 1$ the identification is an isomorphism of $U(2) \times U(3)$-representations: the cell action $A \otimes B$ on the cells and the unit action on $\Con_{1}$ coincide. The pair-level generators of T12 are the one-pair constituents.
\item For $k = 2, 3$ the cell action does not preserve $R_{k}$: the $U(2) \times U(3)$-span of $R_{k}$ under the cell action is $S^{k}\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{k}\C^{3}$, of dimension $(k+1)\binom{3}{k}$, that is $(3,\bar{3})$ at $k = 2$ and $(4,1)$ at $k = 3$, with abelian weight $k$; under the unit action $\Con_{k} = (2, \Lambda^{k}\C^{3})$ has dimension $2\binom{3}{k}$ and weight $3 - 2k$. The same basis states, the filled rows among them, carry different representations under the two actions.
\item Consequently the constituent space is not a subrepresentation of the tensor ledger, and the tensor ledger is the Fock space of the pairs, the multi-pair states of the composite counted cell by cell; it is not the space of the composite's constituents.
\end{enumerate}

standard-model.gauge-algebra-minimal-mas.formal:
The maximal hierarchy of three conformal spinors (the three-twistor hierarchy) $\C^{3} \otimes \C^{3}$ has automorphism algebra $\su(3) \oplus \su(3) \oplus \uu(1)$, of dimension seventeen, which is not the Standard Model's. The minimal massive one is: mass first exists at two constituents (Fact \claimref{mass.mass-symplectic-area-two-twistor-formula}), so matter's \emph{minimal massive} hierarchy is $\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}$, two conformal spinors of three pairs each, and its automorphisms form $(U(2) \times U(3))/U(1)$, with Lie algebra
\begin{equation}
\su(2) \oplus \su(3) \oplus \uu(1),
\end{equation}
of dimension $4 + 9 - 1 = 12 = 3 + 8 + 1$: the Standard Model Lie algebra exactly. The global group is determined by Theorem \claimref{standard-model.global-form-quotient-determined}.

standard-model.global-form-quotient-dete.formal:
The global gauge group of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.gauge-algebra-minimal-massive-composite} is determined exactly. The kernel of the $(U(2) \times U(3))$-action on $\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}$ (numbers and the module's complex structure are read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_{4}\}$, the module read on the tower's floors, where $I_{4}$ commutes with the tower vectors; $I_{2}$ and $I_{3}$ are identity matrices) is $K = \{(\lambda I_{2}, \lambda^{-1} I_{3})\} \cong U(1)$, and
\begin{equation}
(U(2) \times U(3))/K \;\cong\; S(U(2) \times U(3)) \;\cong\; \bigl(SU(2) \times SU(3) \times U(1)\bigr)/\mathbb{Z}_{6}:
\end{equation}
the framework's group is the smallest of the Standard Model's global forms, the full $\mathbb{Z}_{6}$ quotient, not the naive product and not either intermediate quotient. Three consequences. (i) $S(U(2) \times U(3))$ is exactly the subgroup of $SU(5)$ preserving the splitting $\C^{5} = \C^{2} \oplus \C^{3}$, and none of the three proper covers $(SU(2) \times SU(3) \times U(1))/\Gamma$, $\Gamma \subsetneq \mathbb{Z}_{6}$, acts faithfully through it; so the framework's global form is the one that grand unification's embedding requires, and the trace ratio $3/8$ of the section “Unification and the desert” refers to the same normalisation from the other side. (ii) The representations of the group are confined to the $\mathbb{Z}_{6}$ charge lattice: on a representation of the covering product with colour triality $t \in \mathbb{Z}_{3}$, weak parity $\delta \in \mathbb{Z}_{2}$ (the class of the $SU(2)$ content: $1$ on a doublet, $0$ on a singlet or triplet) and abelian weight $q \in \mathbb{Z}$ (the integer such that the $U(1)$ factor $\lambda$ acts by $\lambda^{q}$), descent to the quotient holds if and only if $2t + 3\delta + q \equiv 0 \pmod 6$; writing $Y = q/6$ this is the condition that $t/3 + \delta/2 + Y$ be an integer. The content of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency} lies on it (each of its representations is built as a representation of $S(U(2) \times U(3))$), and the lattice check is printed there. (iii) A structural stance, registered: the lattice forbids representations off it, so a confirmed discovery of, for example, a fractionally charged colour singlet would refute the identification of the gauge group with the automorphism group of minimal massive compositeness, a stance that current searches test \cite{PDG2024}. The stance inherits the conditionality of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.gauge-algebra-minimal-massive-composite}'s premise and is flagged at that level.

standard-model.imported-two-loop-gauge-c.formal:
In $\beta(g_{i}) = \dfrac{g_{i}^{3}}{16\pi^{2}}\Bigl(b_{i} + \dfrac{1}{16\pi^{2}}\sum_{j} B_{ij}\,g_{j}^{2} - \dfrac{c_{i}\,y_{t}^{2}}{16\pi^{2}}\Bigr)$, the two-loop matrix for Weyl fermions in representations $R$ and complex scalars in representations $S$ of a product of simple factors and a $\mathrm{U}(1)$ is
\begin{align*}
B_{ij} \;=\; {}& \delta_{ij}\Bigl(-\tfrac{34}{3}C(G_{i})^{2} + \tfrac{10}{3}C(G_{i})\textstyle\sum_{R} T_{i}(R) + \tfrac{2}{3}C(G_{i})\sum_{S} T_{i}(S)\Bigr) \\
& + 2\textstyle\sum_{R} T_{i}(R)C_{j}(R) + 4\sum_{S} T_{i}(S)C_{j}(S),
\end{align*}
with $T_{i}$ the index weighted by the dimensions of the other factors and $C_{j}$ the quadratic Casimir ($\tfrac35 Y^{2}$ for the $\mathrm{U}(1)$), gauge Yukawas excluded; for a supersymmetric theory with chiral superfields $\Phi$ it is $B_{ij} = \delta_{ij}\bigl(-6C(G_{i})^{2} + 2C(G_{i})\sum_{\Phi} T_{i}\bigr) + 4\sum_{\Phi} T_{i}C_{j}$. The Standard Model's matrix is $\bigl[\begin{smallmatrix} 199/50 & 27/10 & 44/5 \\ 9/10 & 35/6 & 12 \\ 11/10 & 9/2 & -26 \end{smallmatrix}\bigr]$ and the minimal supersymmetric one $\bigl[\begin{smallmatrix} 199/25 & 27/5 & 88/5 \\ 9/5 & 25 & 24 \\ 11/5 & 9 & 14 \end{smallmatrix}\bigr]$; the top-Yukawa coefficients are $c = (\tfrac{17}{10}, \tfrac32, 2)$ and $(\tfrac{26}{5}, 6, 4)$, and $y_{t}$ runs at one loop with $\beta(y_{t}) = \dfrac{y_{t}}{16\pi^{2}}\bigl(\tfrac92 y_{t}^{2} - \tfrac{17}{20}g_{1}^{2} - \tfrac94 g_{2}^{2} - 8g_{3}^{2}\bigr)$ below and $\dfrac{y_{t}}{16\pi^{2}}\bigl(6y_{t}^{2} - \tfrac{13}{15}g_{1}^{2} - 3g_{2}^{2} - \tfrac{16}{3}g_{3}^{2}\bigr)$ above the threshold. \emph{Provenance:} \cite{MachacekVaughn1983, MartinVaughn1994}; the record's central values and uncertainties $\alpha^{-1}_{\mathrm{em}}(M_{Z}) = 127.951$, $\sin^{2}\theta_{W} = 0.23122 \pm 0.00004$, $\alpha_{s} = 0.1180 \pm 0.0009$ from \cite{PDG2024}.

standard-model.majorana-element.formal:
Under Hypothesis (S), let $T = v_{1}v_{2}v_{3}v_{4}v_{5}$, the product of the five creation operators, a grade-five element of the Clifford algebra of the ten. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] $T$ maps $\nu^{c}$, the empty state, to the full state and annihilates every other state, so the form $B(\psi, T\varphi)$ of Lemma \claimref{standard-model.invariant-pairing-five-modes} on the sixteen is the $\nu^{c}\nu^{c}$ entry alone: the right-handed neutrino's Majorana mass;
\item[(2)] $T$ commutes with $\su(3)$, $\su(2)_{L}$ and $Y$, and $[B - L, T] = -2T$; in the compact Pati-Salam algebra, the real span of the $21$, the subalgebra fixing $T$ is twelve-dimensional, exactly $\su(3) \oplus \su(2)_{L} \oplus \uu(1)_{Y}$;
\item[(3)] the orbit of $T$ under the $21$ spans thirty dimensions, on which $\su(2)_{L}$ acts trivially, and $T$ is the highest weight of an $\su(2)_{R}$ triplet: the orbit is the $(10,1,3)$.
\end{enumerate}
So a vev along $T$ breaks Pati-Salam to the Standard Model algebra and gives $\nu^{c}$ its Majorana mass at the same scale. It also breaks the thirteenth generator of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.under-s-composites-state-space-spinor}(3), which lies in $\su(2)_{R} \oplus \su(4)$ as $X = 2T_{3R} - \tfrac32(B - L)$.

standard-model.module-factorises-even-gr.formal:
$\Lambda^{\bullet}(W \oplus R_{2}) = \Lambda^{\bullet}W \otimes \Lambda^{\bullet}R_{2}$ as a module over the complexified two-rung algebra $T_{2} \otimes \C \cong \mathrm{M}_{32}(\C)$, irreducibly, and with the pair modes ordered before the rung modes:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] every vector of $V_{\C}$ acts as $x_{W} \otimes 1$, every product of two vectors of $V_{\C}$ as $X_{W} \otimes 1$, and hence every element of the even ground subalgebra $\Cl^{0}(V_{\C})$, in particular every generator of $\so(2,4)$, as an operator on $\Lambda^{\bullet}W$ tensored with the identity on $\Lambda^{\bullet}R_{2}$;
\item[(2)] every vector of the rung plane acts as $\gamma \otimes y_{R}$ with $\gamma = (-1)^{N_{W}}$ the pair-number grading, so that vectors of $V_{\C}$ and of the rung plane anticommute, while products of two rung vectors act as $1 \otimes Y_{R}$;
\item[(3)] consequently $\so(V_{\C})$ and $\so(R_{2} \oplus \overline{R}_{2})$ commute elementwise inside $\so(V_{\C} \oplus R_{2} \oplus \overline{R}_{2})$, and every element of $\so(V_{\C})$ commutes with the rung number $N_{R}$ and with the rung mixing $\uu(2) = \operatorname{span}\{v_{a}w_{b}\}_{a, b \in \{4, 5\}}$.
\end{enumerate}

standard-model.pair-mixing-not-colour.formal:
Let $U(3)$ be the pair mixing of $W$, the unitary group of the Fock structure $v_{i}^{*} = w_{i}$ on the conformal spinor's (twistor's) own three modes. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $U(3)$ is not a subgroup of the conformal group $SO_{0}(2,4) \cong SU(2,2)/\mathbb{Z}_{2}$: it is compact of dimension $9$, and the maximal compact subgroup has dimension $7$ (Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-maximal-compact-subgroups}). Its Lie algebra does lie in the complexified conformal algebra $\so(6, \C) = \Lambda^{2}V_{\C}$, its generators $v_{i}w_{j}$ being bivectors, so the statement is one about real forms: $\uu(3)$ is a compact subalgebra of $\so(6,\C)$ that no conjugate of $\so(2,4)$ contains.
\item[(ii)] $U(3)$ is not an internal symmetry either: the commutant of $\so(2,4)$ acting on $\Lambda^{\bullet}W$ is two-dimensional, the scalars on $\CS = \Lambda^{\mathrm{even}}W$, the conformal spinor space, and on $\CSm{} = \Lambda^{\mathrm{odd}}W$, so no non-scalar operator on the conformal spinor's Fock space, and in particular no element of $\uu(3)$, commutes with the conformal algebra.
\item[(iii)] Consequently the $U(3)$ of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.frozen-point-pair-mixing} is a Bogoliubov relabelling of the Fock presentation, a change of which conformal spinor is called the vacuum, and its failure to fix the frozen point says that rotating a conformal spinor into a different one changes the momentum, which is true and harmless. The colour group of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.gauge-algebra-minimal-massive-composite} acts on the hierarchy bundle $\mathcal{H}$, a label copy of the pair space carried alongside the conformal spinor bundle (the twistor bundle), commuting with the conformal connection by construction (Proposition \claimref{standard-model.connection-forced}); the frozen point is insensitive to it. The sentence ``mass breaks colour'' identifies the two copies of $\C^{3}$, the conformal spinor's own annihilator and its label, and is a category error rather than a result. Theorems \claimref{standard-model.moment-map-grip-mass-are-insensitive} and \claimref{standard-model.grip-mass-two-rung-module} need neither identification: their proofs use the slot count and the nullity of a single conformal spinor's momentum, and nothing about the pair mixing.
\end{enumerate}

standard-model.proton-condition-candidat.formal:
Under Hypothesis (S), at the inputs of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.spectrum-run}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] the three rows that Proposition \claimref{standard-model.table-exact} finds admissible, the wino, the wino with the bino and the higgsinos, and the supersymmetric content without the gluino, have $M_{U} = 2.5$, $1.6$ and $1.5 \times 10^{14}$ GeV and lifetimes of about $2 \times 10^{29}$, $3 \times 10^{28}$ and $2 \times 10^{28}$ yr, short of the limit by five to six orders of magnitude: none meets the proton condition;
\item[(2)] the segment of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.four-supersymmetric-splits}(d) meets the condition from its near-degenerate end down to $(f, f') = (0.868, 0.759)$, where the light set sits at $6 \times 10^{3}$ GeV, the heavy set at $1.9 \times 10^{5}$ GeV and $M_{U} = 5.6 \times 10^{15}$ GeV, and fails it beyond, so that the end with the electroweak-inos at $7 \times 10^{8}$ GeV is excluded;
\item[(3)] the supersymmetric row at two loops (Proposition \claimref{standard-model.supersymmetric-row-two-loops}), with $M_{U} = 1.1 \times 10^{16}$ GeV and $\alpha_{U}^{-1} = 25.7$, has a lifetime of about $3 \times 10^{35}$ yr and meets the condition.
\end{enumerate}

standard-model.quarks-leptons-two-level-.formal:
Under $S(U(2) \times U(3))$, with $Y$ the hypercharge just defined:
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Unconditional.) The pair-level generators, the cells of the tensor state space, transform as $(2, 3)$ with abelian weight $q = 1$, so $Y = 1/6$: the quark doublet's representation. A single pair has one unit of hypercharge.
\item (Under Hypothesis (S).) The states with one slot mode occupied and the pair modes in an $SU(3)$-invariant configuration are $\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{0}W$, weight $3$ ($Y = +1/2$, total mode number one, odd), and $\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{3}W$, weight $-3$ ($Y = -1/2$, total mode number four, even); both transform as $(2, 1)$. Without (S) neither exists in the state space, whose only row-filling states form the quadruplet $(4,1)$ of weight $+3$.
\item (Unconditional as a computation on the Fock space of a single conformal spinor, with the pair-slot factor acting alone; that Fock space is not a subspace of the tensor state space, and under (S) it is the row $j = 0$.) In the sector of a single conformal spinor (the single-twistor sector) $\Lambda^{\bullet}W = \Lambda^{\bullet}\C^{3}$, the grade-$k$ states transform as $\Lambda^{k}\C^{3}$, that is as $1, 3, \bar{3}, 1$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, 3$, with weight $q = -2k$: $Y = 0, -1/3, -2/3, -1$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, under (S), the fermion content of the composite at the particle handedness (Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency}) is
\begin{equation}
(2,3)_{1/6} \oplus (2,1)_{-1/2},
\end{equation}
quark doublet plus lepton doublet, with colour neutrality of the lepton the statement that a state with its pair modes full is an $SU(3)$ singlet: a lepton is a complete conformal spinor, one slot with the pair system full, while a quark is one slot with one pair.

standard-model.raw-representation-conten.formal:
Under $\su(2) \oplus \su(3) \oplus \uu(1)$, with the abelian charge the pair number, the full Fock space of the minimal massive hierarchy (numbers and the module's complex structure are read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_{4}\}$, the module read on the tower's floors, where $I_{4}$ commutes with the tower vectors) decomposes as
\begin{equation}
\Lambda^{\bullet}(\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}) = (1,1) \oplus (2,3) \oplus \bigl[(3,\bar{3}) \oplus (1,6)\bigr] \oplus \bigl[(4,1) \oplus (2,8)\bigr] \oplus \bigl[(3,3) \oplus (1,\bar{6})\bigr] \oplus (2,\bar{3}) \oplus (1,1),
\end{equation}
graded by pair number $0$ through $6$; and the sector of a single conformal spinor (the single-twistor sector, massless) decomposes as $\Lambda^{\bullet}\C^{3} = 1 \oplus 3 \oplus \bar{3} \oplus 1$, graded $0$ through $3$.

proofs.main:
(1) By Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-cauchy-decomposition-exterior-power}, $\Lambda^{n}(\C^{2} \oplus \C^{3}) = \bigoplus_{j+k=n}\Lambda^{j}\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{k}\C^{3}$, and $(A, B) = (\lambda^{3}a, \lambda^{-2}b)$ acts on $\Lambda^{j}\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{k}\C^{3}$ as $\lambda^{3j}\lambda^{-2k}\,\Lambda^{j}a \otimes \Lambda^{k}b$. The twelve components, with $\Lambda^{0}\C^{2} = \Lambda^{2}\C^{2} = 1$, $\Lambda^{1}\C^{2} = 2$, and $\Lambda^{0,1,2,3}\C^{3} = 1, 3, \bar{3}, 1$ under $SU(2) \times SU(3)$, with weight $3j - 2k$ and $Y = (3j-2k)/6$:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
j \backslash k & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\ \hline
0 & (1,1)_{0} & (1,3)_{-1/3} & (1,\bar{3})_{-2/3} & (1,1)_{-1} \\
1 & (2,1)_{1/2} & (2,3)_{1/6} & (2,\bar{3})_{-1/6} & (2,1)_{-1/2} \\
2 & (1,1)_{1} & (1,3)_{2/3} & (1,\bar{3})_{1/3} & (1,1)_{0}
\end{array}
\]
The even components ($j + k$ even) are $(0,0) = \nu^{c}$, $(0,2) = u^{c}$, $(1,1) = Q$, $(1,3) = L$, $(2,0) = e^{c}$, $(2,2) = d^{c}$, exactly the six representations of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency} with their hypercharges, and irreducibles of $S(U(2) \times U(3))$ are determined by content and weight (proof of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.chirality-two-halves-spinor-module-conjugation}), so the even part is the derived generation; its dimension is $1 + 3 + 6 + 2 + 1 + 3 = 16$. The odd components are $(0,1) = (1,3)_{-1/3}$, $(0,3) = (1,1)_{-1}$, $(1,0) = (2,1)_{1/2}$, $(1,2) = (2,\bar{3})_{-1/6}$, $(2,1) = (1,3)_{2/3}$, $(2,3) = (1,1)_{0}$, the duals of $d^{c}$, $e^{c}$, $L$, $Q$, $u^{c}$, $\nu^{c}$ respectively (conjugate content, opposite weight); the map $(j, k) \mapsto (2 - j, 3 - k)$, the Hodge star of the exterior algebra, realises the duality and negates the weight, $3(2-j) - 2(3-k) = -(3j - 2k)$. The $(2,0)$ entry is $\Lambda^{2}\C^{2} = \det$, the slot factor of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.mass-bracket-charged-singlet}. (2) is Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-fock-representation-clifford-algebra-bogoliubov} with $n = 5$, the five modes of Hypothesis (S) (\claimref{standard-model.hyp-s-slot-statistics}), together with the observation that the split-preserving, number-preserving automorphisms of the five modes are the unitaries of $\C^{2} \oplus \C^{3}$ preserving the summands, $U(2) \times U(3)$; the parenthesis is the "determinant phase" clause of that Fact, and the twist $\det^{-1/2}$ is trivial where $\det(A \oplus B) = 1$. (3) Over the even components, the generator's values are $\nu^{c}$: $0$; $u^{c}$: $2\beta$; $Q$: $\alpha + \beta$; $L$: $\alpha + 3\beta$; $e^{c}$: $2\alpha$; $d^{c}$: $2\alpha + 2\beta$. With Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-anomaly-coefficients}'s sums in the form used in the proof of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency}(3), $Q$ entering the $SU(3)$ sum twice and the gravitational and cubic sums six times, $L$ twice, and the singlets by their colour multiplicity: $[SU(3)]^{2}U(1) = 2(\alpha + \beta) + 2\beta + (2\alpha + 2\beta) = 4\alpha + 6\beta$; $[SU(2)]^{2}U(1) = 3(\alpha + \beta) + (\alpha + 3\beta) = 4\alpha + 6\beta$; gravitational $= 6(\alpha + \beta) + 3 \cdot 2\beta + 3(2\alpha + 2\beta) + 2(\alpha + 3\beta) + 2\alpha = 16\alpha + 24\beta$; cubic $= 6(\alpha + \beta)^{3} + 3(2\beta)^{3} + 3(2\alpha + 2\beta)^{3} + 2(\alpha + 3\beta)^{3} + (2\alpha)^{3}$, which at $(3, -2)$ is $6 - 192 + 24 - 54 + 216 = 0$, at $(1, 1)$ is $48 + 24 + 192 + 128 + 8 = 400$, and at $(1, -1)$ is $0 - 24 + 0 - 16 + 8 = -32$. The three linear sums are multiples of $2\alpha + 3\beta$, so vanish together exactly on the line through $(3,-2)$; on that line the cubic sum is a cubic in one variable vanishing at $(3,-2)$, hence identically. The script evaluates all four sums for every integer pair $(\alpha, \beta) \in [-6, 6]^{2}$ and finds the three linear ones vanishing exactly at the multiples of $(3, -2)$. For (b): the twist subtracts $\tfrac12(2\alpha + 3\beta)$ from every entry, which removes the three linear sums exactly, since they count four, four and sixteen entries; the cubic and the two mixed sums become polynomials of degree at most three in each of $\alpha$ and $\beta$, which the script finds vanishing on a five-by-five grid of rational points, hence identically. That nothing in $\mathrm{Spin}(10)$ is anomalous on the $16$ is also the standard statement that $SO(10)$ has no cubic invariant. The values of $X$ and the identity $X = 2Y - \tfrac52(B - L)$ are read off the table of (1), species by species. The script builds the hypercharge generator as an exact matrix on all thirty-two dimensions of $\Lambda^{\bullet}(\C^{2} \oplus \C^{3})$, reads off the weight on each component, confirms that the even components' (content, weight) multiset is the derived spectrum's and that the odd components' is its conjugate, and confirms that the Hodge star negates the weight on all twelve components.

standard-model.weak-angle-over-derived-g.formal:
Over one generation of the content of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency}, quark and lepton doublets and the four singlets (the lepton under Hypothesis (S)), with $Q = T_{3} + Y$,
\begin{equation}
\frac{\sum T_{3}^{2}}{\sum Q^{2}} \;=\; \frac{2}{2 + \tfrac{10}{3}} \;=\; \frac{3}{8},
\end{equation}
and this is $\sin^{2}\theta_{W}$ at the unification scale under the hypothesis of Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-one-loop-running-trace-ratio}(b), the classic value, here over derived rather than assumed content. The value rests on (S) with the lepton doublet, which contributes $\tfrac12$ to each sum: over the unconditional content alone, the quark doublet and the four singlets, the same ratio is $9/26$. Over the fibre $\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}$ alone the ratio is $9/10$, unconditionally, and over the two doublets alone $3/4$, again under (S). Comparison with the observed $0.2312$ requires renormalisation-group running from the unification scale (the structural scale $M_{U}$), which Proposition \claimref{standard-model.spectrum-run} performs.

synthesis.colour-light-content-proton-ce.formal:
Work at one loop in the normalisation of Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-one-loop-running-trace-ratio}(a), with $t = \ln(\mu/M_{Z})/2\pi$, the Standard Model coefficients $b = (\tfrac{41}{10}, -\tfrac{19}{6}, -7)$ below every threshold and the record values $a_{i} = a_{i}(M_{Z})$ of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.spectrum-run}, so that content $\Dl^{(k)}$ switched on at thresholds $t_{k} \ge 0$ gives $a_{i}(t) = a_{i} - b_{i}t - \sum_{k} \Dl^{(k)}_{i}(t - t_{k})_{+}$. Put $t_{13} := (a_{1} - a_{3})/(b_{1} - b_{3})$, where $a_{1}$ and $a_{3}$ cross with no content added ($M_{13} = M_{Z}e^{2\pi t_{13}} = 2.5 \times 10^{14}$ GeV). Suppose every threshold's content has $\Dl^{(k)}_{3} \ge 0$ and $\Dl^{(k)}_{1} \ge \Dl^{(k)}_{3}$: no heavier in colour than in hypercharge. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] every point with $a_{1}(t) = a_{3}(t)$, in particular any meeting of the three couplings, has $t \le t_{13}$ and $a_{3}(t) \le a_{3} + 7t_{13}$; $t = t_{13}$ exactly when $\Dl^{(k)}_{1} = \Dl^{(k)}_{3}$ at every threshold below the crossing, and content with $\Dl^{(k)}_{1} = \Dl^{(k)}_{3} = 0$ below the crossing, the wino for one, attains both bounds;
\item[(ii)] hence $M_{U} \le M_{13}$ and $\alpha_{U}^{-1} \le a_{3} + 7t_{13} = 40.4$, and the proton figure of Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-proton-condition} obeys $\tfrac{\alpha_{U}^{-1}}{45}\bigl(M_{U}/10^{15}\,\mathrm{GeV}\bigr)^{2} < \tfrac{1}{10}$, against the condition's $5.2\sqrt{15} > 20$: no such content, at any number of thresholds, meets the proton condition, and it misses by a factor above $200$ in that figure, about $10^{5}$ in lifetime;
\item[(iii)] the three spectra that Proposition \claimref{standard-model.table-exact}(d) finds admissible, $\Dl = (0, \tfrac43, 0)$, $(\tfrac25, 2, 0)$ and $(\tfrac52, \tfrac{25}{6}, 2)$, satisfy the hypothesis, so clause (e) of that proposition follows from (ii) with an argument of its own: they fail the proton condition because none is heavier in colour than in hypercharge.
\end{enumerate}
Colour singlets and complete multiplets always satisfy the hypothesis, and a colour triplet does exactly when $|Y| \ge \sqrt{5/18}$; a gluino ($\Dl_{1} - \Dl_{3} = -2$) and $Q$- or $d^{c}$-type triplets do not. So a one-loop spectrum that meets the proton condition (physical content has $\Dl_{3} \ge 0$) must carry, at some threshold, content with $\Dl_{3} > \Dl_{1}$.

synthesis.even-slots-need-a-spectator-ge.formal:
Let $F = \Cl(5,5)$ be a floor spanned by five rungs of the tower, with null pairs $(v_{k}, w_{k})$ satisfying the canonical anticommutation relations as vectors (Proposition \claimref{tower.ladder-pairs-satisfy-car-vectors}); let rungs $1, 2, 3$ carry the composite's pair modes, as in Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-pair-systems-two-chirality-halves}, with pair algebra $P$ and pair parity $\Gamma_{W} = \prod_{k \le 3}(1 - 2v_{k}w_{k})$. Let $s_{1}, s_{2} \in F_{\C}$ ($F_{\C} = F \oplus I_{4}F$, $\C = \mathrm{span}\{1, I_{4}\}$, the unit being the field theory's $I_{4}$, which commutes with every tower vector) be slot operators that are (a) homogeneous of tower parity $\pi$, (b) two-level, $s_{i}^{2} = 0$, $s_{i}s_{i}^{*} + s_{i}^{*}s_{i} = 1$, (c) carried into two-level operators by every $A \in U(2)$ acting on their span, and (d) such that each slot operator commutes with every pair operator, or each anticommutes with every one. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $s_{1}, s_{2}$ satisfy the canonical anticommutation relations among themselves; commuting two-level slots violate (c);
\item[(ii)] the commutant $P'$ of $P$ in $F$ is spanned by $16$ blades, of which $8$ are even and span a subalgebra; $s_{i}$ or $s_{i}\Gamma_{W}$ lies in $P'$ and has parity $\pi$; two canonical anticommutation modes generate a $16$-dimensional algebra; hence $\pi = 1$: inside $F$ every such pair of slots is odd;
\item[(iii)] with $\pi = 1$, after replacing $s_{i}$ by $s_{i}\Gamma_{W}$ if they commute with the pairs (which changes each state $s_{J}^{*}b_{K}^{*}\lvert 0\rangle$ only by $(-1)^{jk}$), the five modes satisfy the canonical anticommutation relations, the states built on their joint vacuum span $32$ dimensions, $U(2) \times U(3)$ acts as $\Lambda^{\bullet}(A \oplus B)$, and the tower grading, conjugation by the pseudoscalar of $F$ (the only blade anticommuting with all ten generators), takes the value $(-1)^{j + k}$ on the component with $j$ slots and $k$ pairs occupied relative to the vacuum: every clause of Hypothesis (S), with the tower grading as chirality;
\item[(iv)] in $\Cl(6,5)$, the floor with one further generator $g$, $g^{2} = 1$, the even operators $s_{k} = v_{k}g$ ($k = 4, 5$) satisfy (b), (c) and (d).
\end{enumerate}
So the tower parity of two-level, $U(2)$-covariant slots is fixed by whether the slot operators may use a generator outside the composite's own five rungs: without such a spectator they are odd and Hypothesis (S) follows with the tower grading as chirality; with one, even slots exist, and for them the tower grading is $(-1)^{k}$ on the component $(j, k)$, not the chirality (S) declares. The statistics no-go of Proposition \claimref{field-theory.no-canonical-commutation-relations-tower} excludes neither.

synthesis.georgi-jarlskog-majorana-momen.formal:
Under Hypothesis (S), on the five-mode module with the pairing $B$ of Lemma \claimref{standard-model.invariant-pairing-five-modes}, let $T = v_{1}v_{2}v_{3}v_{4}v_{5}$ be the Majorana element of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.majorana-element}, let $\omega = \sum_{i \le 3} v_{i} \wedge w_{i}$ be the pair-number generator, so that $B - L = -\tfrac23\,\omega$, and let $\chi$ and $T_{\mathrm{GJ}} = w_{4}(B - L)\chi$ be as in Proposition \claimref{families.doublet-direction-module-georgi-jarlskog-clebsch}. Define the moment map $\mu(T)$ as the orthogonal projection, for the trace form, of $[T, T^{\dagger}]$ onto the span of the bivectors of the ten, and write $\mu_{P}$ and $\mu_{R}$ for its components along the bivectors of the pair space and of the rung space, the split $\so(6) \oplus \so(4)$ that the slot/pair split selects (Proposition \claimref{standard-model.automorphisms-preserve-slot-pair-split-are}). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\mu(T) = \tfrac18\sum_{i \le 5} v_{i} \wedge w_{i} = \tfrac18 X$, with $X = 2T_{3R} - \tfrac32(B - L)$ the thirteenth generator of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.under-s-composites-state-space-spinor}(3); it has no component mixing the pair and rung spaces, and $\mu_{P} = \tfrac18\,\omega = -\tfrac{3}{16}(B - L)$, $\mu_{R} = \tfrac14 T_{3R}$;
\item[(ii)] for every polynomial $p$, the symmetric part of the mass form of $w_{4}\,p(\mu(T))$ on the sixteen is a multiple of the $10$ form: the unsplit moment map gives $|y_{e}| = |y_{d}|$ only;
\item[(iii)] $w_{4}\,\mu_{P} = -\tfrac{3}{16}\,w_{4}(B - L)$, the grade-three direction of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.grade-three-georgi-jarlskog-direction}, with antisymmetric mass form;
\item[(iv)] $w_{4}\,\mu_{R}\,\mu_{P} = \tfrac{3}{256}\,w_{4}(B - L)(\chi - 1) = \tfrac{3}{256}\bigl(T_{\mathrm{GJ}} - w_{4}(B - L)\bigr)$. Its mass form has symmetric part $\tfrac{3}{256}$ times the Georgi-Jarlskog form, ratio $|y_{e}/y_{d}| = 3$, and antisymmetric part $-\tfrac{3}{256}$ times the grade-three form, the two equal in modulus entry by entry; the operator annihilates the doublet sector $N_{R} = 1$.
\end{enumerate}
So the pair-sector $\uu(1)$ current that $T_{\mathrm{GJ}}$ lacks is the Pati-Salam pair component of the moment map of the Majorana element, and one composite of the rung vector with the two components of that moment map carries both the $126$ term of the second family and the grade-three term between families, with equal moduli. The coupling constant of such a composite, and a dynamics selecting it, are not supplied.

synthesis.georgi-jarlskog-square-of-majo.formal:
Under Hypothesis (S), on the five-mode module with the pairing of Lemma \claimref{standard-model.invariant-pairing-five-modes}, let $T = v_{1}v_{2}v_{3}v_{4}v_{5}$ be the Majorana element of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.majorana-element}, $T^{\dagger}$ its adjoint, $\mu = \mu(T)$, $\mu_{P}$, $\mu_{R}$ its moment map and the Pati-Salam components of Proposition \claimref{synthesis.georgi-jarlskog-majorana-moment-map}, and $T_{\mathrm{GJ}} = w_{4}(B - L)\chi$ as in Proposition \claimref{families.doublet-direction-module-georgi-jarlskog-clebsch}. Expand operators in the monomials of the ten Majorana generators $a_{i} = v_{i} + w_{i}$, $b_{i} = v_{i} - w_{i}$, graded by degree. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $TT^{\dagger}$ is the projector onto the full state; its components of degree $0$, $2$ and $4$ are $\tfrac{1}{32}$, $\tfrac12\mu$ and $G_{4} = 4\mu^{2} - \tfrac{5}{64}$;
\item[(ii)] the part $X_{4}$ of $G_{4}$ whose monomials contain generators of both the pair and the rung modes is $X_{4} = 8\,\mu_{R}\mu_{P}$; it commutes with $\su(2)_{L}$ and with $B - L$ but not with $v_{4}v_{5}$, so it lies in the $(15,1,3)$ of the degree-four component;
\item[(iii)] $w_{4}X_{4} = \tfrac{3}{32}\bigl(T_{\mathrm{GJ}} - w_{4}(B - L)\bigr)$, whose mass form has symmetric part $\tfrac{3}{32}$ times the Georgi-Jarlskog form (ratio $|y_{e}/y_{d}| = 3$) and antisymmetric part $-\tfrac{3}{32}$ times the grade-three form;
\item[(iv)] $w_{4}G_{4}$ has no symmetric form: the symmetric part of its mass form is the $10$ form with multiple zero (as Theorem \claimref{synthesis.georgi-jarlskog-is-rung-vector-times-pair-part-of-majorana-square}(iv) also finds), since $G_{4}$ takes opposite values $\mp\tfrac{1}{16}$ on the two states of each pair the rung vector couples;
\item[(v)] under $T \mapsto cT$, $X_{4} \mapsto |c|^{2}X_{4}$, while $w_{4}\mu_{R}\mu_{P} \mapsto |c|^{4}\,w_{4}\mu_{R}\mu_{P}$.
\end{enumerate}
So the composite that carries the Georgi-Jarlskog factor is, up to the factor $8$, the $(15,1,3)$ part of the square $TT^{\dagger}$ of the Majorana element: it already appears at second order in a vev $c$ along $T$, not only at fourth order through the moment map, while the Majorana mass of $\nu^{c}$ is linear in $c$. Reading the composite as this part of the square, its strength relative to the rung vector's $10$ term is of order $|c|^{2}$; which polynomial in $T$ and $T^{\dagger}$ the dynamics selects is not derived, and the coefficient is not fixed.

