synthesis.imported-general-quartic-runni.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(General quartic running.)} For real scalars $\phi_{a}$ with potential $V = \tfrac{1}{4!}\lambda_{abcd}\phi_{a}\phi_{b}\phi_{c}\phi_{d}$, in a gauge theory with gauge coupling $g$ and Yukawa couplings to fermions, in a mass-independent scheme, $16\pi^{2}\beta_{abcd}^{(1)} = \Lambda^{2}_{abcd} - 8\kappa H_{abcd} + 2\kappa\Lambda^{Y}_{abcd} - 3g^{2}\Lambda^{S}_{abcd} + 3g^{4}A_{abcd}$ with $\Lambda^{2}_{abcd} = \tfrac18\sum_{\mathrm{perm}}\lambda_{abef}\lambda_{efcd}$, and the two-loop terms built from the quartics alone are $(16\pi^{2})^{2}\beta_{abcd}^{(2)} \supset \tfrac{1}{12}\sum_{\mathrm{perm}}\Lambda^{2}_{af}\lambda_{fbcd} - \bar{\Lambda}^{3}_{abcd}$, with $\Lambda^{2}_{ab} = \tfrac16\lambda_{acde}\lambda_{bcde}$ and $\bar{\Lambda}^{3}_{abcd} = \tfrac14\sum_{\mathrm{perm}}\lambda_{abef}\lambda_{cegh}\lambda_{dfgh}$ \cite{MachacekVaughn1985, LuoWangXiao2003, Schienbein2019}.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Two doublets.)} For two doublets $\Phi_{1}$, $\Phi_{2}$ of hypercharge $\tfrac12$ with $V \supset \tfrac12\lambda_{1}(\Phi_{1}^{\dagger}\Phi_{1})^{2} + \tfrac12\lambda_{2}(\Phi_{2}^{\dagger}\Phi_{2})^{2} + \lambda_{3}(\Phi_{1}^{\dagger}\Phi_{1})(\Phi_{2}^{\dagger}\Phi_{2}) + \lambda_{4}(\Phi_{1}^{\dagger}\Phi_{2})(\Phi_{2}^{\dagger}\Phi_{1}) + [\tfrac12\lambda_{5}(\Phi_{1}^{\dagger}\Phi_{2})^{2} + \mathrm{h.c.}]$ (a discrete symmetry forbidding the other quartics), $\Phi_{2}$ coupled to the top with $y_{t}$ and $\Phi_{1}$ to the bottom with $y_{b}$ (type II), gauge couplings $g$, $g'$ (non-unified normalisation) and $w = 3(3g^{2} + g'^{2})$, at one loop
\begin{align*}
16\pi^{2}\beta_{\lambda_{1}} &= 12\lambda_{1}^{2} + 4\lambda_{3}^{2} + 4\lambda_{3}\lambda_{4} + 2\lambda_{4}^{2} + 2|\lambda_{5}|^{2} + \tfrac34(3g^{4} + g'^{4} + 2g^{2}g'^{2}) - w\lambda_{1} + 12\lambda_{1}y_{b}^{2} - 12y_{b}^{4},\\
16\pi^{2}\beta_{\lambda_{3}} &= (\lambda_{1} + \lambda_{2})(6\lambda_{3} + 2\lambda_{4}) + 4\lambda_{3}^{2} + 2\lambda_{4}^{2} + 2|\lambda_{5}|^{2} + \tfrac34(3g^{4} + g'^{4} - 2g^{2}g'^{2}) - w\lambda_{3} + 6\lambda_{3}(y_{t}^{2} + y_{b}^{2}) - 12y_{t}^{2}y_{b}^{2},\\
16\pi^{2}\beta_{\lambda_{4}} &= 2(\lambda_{1} + \lambda_{2})\lambda_{4} + 8\lambda_{3}\lambda_{4} + 4\lambda_{4}^{2} + 8|\lambda_{5}|^{2} + 3g^{2}g'^{2} - w\lambda_{4} + 6\lambda_{4}(y_{t}^{2} + y_{b}^{2}) + 12y_{t}^{2}y_{b}^{2},\\
16\pi^{2}\beta_{\lambda_{5}} &= \lambda_{5}\bigl(2(\lambda_{1} + \lambda_{2}) + 8\lambda_{3} + 12\lambda_{4} - w + 6(y_{t}^{2} + y_{b}^{2})\bigr),
\end{align*}
and $\beta_{\lambda_{2}}$ is $\beta_{\lambda_{1}}$ with $\lambda_{1} \to \lambda_{2}$, $y_{b} \to y_{t}$ \cite{HaberHempfling1993, FerreiraJones2009, Branco2012}.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.imported-long-lived-charged-pa.formal:
Searches for heavy charged particles that cross the detectors before decaying, identified by anomalous ionisation energy loss and long time of flight, set $95$ per cent confidence lower mass limits on long-lived scalar tau leptons (staus):
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] CMS, with $5.0$ fb$^{-1}$ at $\sqrt{s} = 7$ TeV and $18.8$ fb$^{-1}$ at $\sqrt{s} = 8$ TeV, tracker plus time-of-flight analysis: staus produced directly in pairs are excluded below $339$ GeV, and with direct and indirect production in the gauge-mediated benchmark below $500$ GeV; for directly produced staus the observed cross-section limit is $0.16$ of the predicted cross-section at the lowest tabulated mass, $126$ GeV;
\item[(ii)] ATLAS, with $36.1$ fb$^{-1}$ at $\sqrt{s} = 13$ TeV: directly pair-produced staus are excluded below $430$ GeV.
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{CMS2013LongLivedCharged} (Section 7 and Table 7), \cite{ATLAS2019LongLivedCharged} (abstract).

synthesis.imported-two-doublet-scalon-su.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(The two-doublet sum rule.)} In the classically scale-invariant model with two Higgs doublets of hypercharge $\tfrac12$, radiative breaking along a flat direction (Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-coleman-weinberg-gildener-weinberg-scalon-mass}) makes the scalon the Standard-Model-like Higgs $h$, and the scalon formula becomes a sum rule on the extra scalars $\mathcal{H} = H, A, H^{\pm}$ (the charged one counted twice): $8\pi^{2}v^{2}m_{h}^{2} = 6M_{W}^{4} + 3M_{Z}^{4} + \sum_{\mathcal H}M_{\mathcal H}^{4} - 12m_{t}^{4}$ up to the lighter fermions, which for $m_{h} = 125$ GeV gives $\bigl(\sum_{\mathcal H}M_{\mathcal H}^{4}\bigr)^{1/4} = 540$ GeV \cite{LeePilaftsis2012, LaneShepherd2018}.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Light neutrino species.)} The invisible width of the $Z$ boson, measured at its resonance, corresponds to $N_{\nu} = 2.9840 \pm 0.0082$ light neutrino species \cite{LEPSLD2006}.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.imported-two-loop-yukawa-gauge.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For a renormalisable gauge theory with simple gauge group $G$ of dimension $d(G)$ and coupling $g$, fermions in a representation $F$ and real scalars in a representation $S$, with Yukawa matrices $Y^{a}$ (one for each real scalar $\phi_{a}$), and with the group invariants $C_{2}$, $S_{2}$ in the notation of the third reference,
\begin{align*}
\beta(g) \;=\; {}& -\frac{g^{3}}{(4\pi)^{2}}\Bigl\{\tfrac{11}{3}C_{2}(G) - \tfrac43\kappa S_{2}(F) - \tfrac16 S_{2}(S) + \frac{2\kappa}{(4\pi)^{2}}Y_{4}(F)\Bigr\} \\
& - \frac{g^{5}}{(4\pi)^{4}}\Bigl\{\tfrac{34}{3}C_{2}(G)^{2} - \kappa\bigl[4C_{2}(F) + \tfrac{20}{3}C_{2}(G)\bigr]S_{2}(F) - \bigl[2C_{2}(S) + \tfrac13 C_{2}(G)\bigr]S_{2}(S)\Bigr\},
\end{align*}
with $Y_{4}(F) = \frac{1}{d(G)}\Tr\bigl[C_{2}(F)\,Y^{a}Y^{a\dagger}\bigr]$ and $\kappa = \tfrac12$ for two-component and $1$ for four-component fermions. The Yukawa couplings enter the gauge running first at two loops, through the term $-2\kappa\,g^{3}Y_{4}(F)/(4\pi)^{4}$.
\item[(b)] For a product of simple factors and $\mathrm{U}(1)$s, the Yukawa term of the coupling $g_{i}$ is the same expression with $C_{2}(F)$ replaced by the quadratic Casimir of the factor $i$ and $d(G)$ by $d(G_{i})$ (with the chosen $\mathrm{U}(1)$ normalisation).
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{MachacekVaughn1983, MachacekVaughn1984}; \cite[Eq.~(30)]{LuoWangXiao2003}.

proofs.main:
(i) A transposition $P$ acts on the antisymmetric matrices $e_{i} \wedge e_{j}$ by permuting the indices and changing the sign when it reverses their order. For the exchange $P_{12}$ of $e_{1}, e_{2}$: $e_{1} \wedge e_{2} \mapsto -e_{1} \wedge e_{2}$ and $e_{1} \wedge e_{3} \leftrightarrow e_{2} \wedge e_{3}$, so the invariants are the multiples of $(e_{1} + e_{2}) \wedge e_{3}$. The exchange $P_{23}$ sends $(e_{1} + e_{2}) \wedge e_{3}$ to $(e_{1} + e_{3}) \wedge e_{2} 
eq (e_{1} + e_{2}) \wedge e_{3}$, so no nonzero antisymmetric matrix is $S_{3}$-invariant (the antisymmetric matrices carry the standard representation plus the sign representation, and no trivial one). Writing $e_{1} + e_{2} = 	frac23 u' + 	frac13 s$ and $e_{3} = 	frac13 u' - 	frac13 s$ gives $(e_{1} + e_{2}) \wedge e_{3} = -	frac13\,u' \wedge s$; and $(u' \wedge s)a = u'(s \cdot a) - s(u' \cdot a) = 0$. For symmetric matrices, the $S_{3}$ statement is Lemma \claimref{neutrino.invariant-symmetric-matrices}; under $P_{12}$ the six symmetric basis matrices fall into the fixed ones $E_{11} + E_{22}$, $E_{33}$, $E_{12} + E_{21}$, and the pairs $\{E_{13} + E_{31}, E_{23} + E_{32}\}$, $\{E_{11}, E_{22}\}$, giving four invariants, and $u'u'^{\mathsf{T}}$, $ss^{\mathsf{T}}$, $aa^{\mathsf{T}}$, $u's^{\mathsf{T}} + su'^{\mathsf{T}}$ are four independent $P_{12}$-invariant symmetric matrices. The check computes the invariant dimensions $0, 1, 2, 4$ as exact kernels over all group elements.

(ii) $\J a = 0$ and $e_{3} \cdot a = 0$, so $(\J + \varepsilon e_{3}e_{3}^{\mathsf{T}})a = 0$ for every $\varepsilon$, while $(\J + \varepsilon e_{3}e_{3}^{\mathsf{T}})s = -2\varepsilon e_{3} 
eq 0$ for $\varepsilon 
eq 0$. On the invariant plane spanned by $u'$ and $s$ the matrix has two nonzero eigenvalues for small $\varepsilon 
e 0$, one near $3$ (the third family, mostly $u'$) and one of order $\varepsilon$ (the second, mostly $s$), and $a$ stays massless until $S_{2}$ is broken. A $120$ coupling is antisymmetric in the family indices (Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-spinor-bilinears}); invariant under the residual $S_{2}$ it is a multiple of $u' \wedge s$ by (i), which pairs the third and second families and annihilates the first: since $u' \wedge s$ spans the antisymmetric matrices of the plane of $u'$ and $s$, it is a multiple of $t_{3} \wedge t_{2}$ for the mass eigenvectors $t_{3}, t_{2}$ of that plane, whatever their mixing. With $S_{3}$ unbroken it vanishes.

(iii) By Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-clebsch-structure-seesaw}, $M_{d} - M_{e} = 4w_{d}f + (c_{d} - c_{e})h$, with the $10$s cancelling; $f$ is symmetric and $h$ antisymmetric (Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-spinor-bilinears}), so $\mathrm{Sym}(M_{d} - M_{e}) = 4w_{d}f$, and $M_{R} = v_{R}f$ (the same lemma, in its general form with the up sector and Premise (EW), is the first clause of the result filed alongside, synthesis.majorana-scale-is-126-doublet-vev). If $M_{R} = \alpha\Pi + M_{3}\J/3$, the form Lemma \claimref{neutrino.breaking-relation} takes (with $\alpha, M_{3} 
eq 0$), then $\mathrm{Sym}(M_{d} - M_{e}) = (4w_{d}/v_{R})(\alpha\Pi + M_{3}\J/3)$, which is $S_{3}$-invariant, has eigenvalue $4w_{d}M_{3}/v_{R}$ on $u'$ and the single eigenvalue $4w_{d}\alpha/v_{R}$ on all of $u'^{\perp}$ (Lemma \claimref{neutrino.invariant-symmetric-matrices}). A symmetric matrix supported on one light state, such as $ss^{\mathsf{T}}$, is moved by $S_{3}$ and so is not of this form; in the $126$'s quark-lepton splitting the two light families therefore enter alike, and the second family's factor $3$ cannot be a $126$ entry on that family. So the Georgi-Jarlskog texture (a $126$ entry on the second family alone) is unavailable; a factor the $126$ supplies to the second family is shared by the first, which then has to meet its own relation $m_{e}/m_{d} \approx 	frac13$ with the same $126$ entry. This does not by itself exclude every fit with a family-blind $126$; the route that remains without that sharing is the $10$s together with the $120$'s antisymmetric coupling, through mixing with the third family, which is the first row of \claimref{standard-model.fits-record-level}'s table. The check verifies the $S_{3}$-invariance of $\mathrm{Sym}(M_{d} - M_{e})$ with generic complex $10$, $120$ and vevs, the two eigenvalues on $u'$, $s$ and $a$, and that $ss^{\mathsf{T}}$ is not $S_{3}$-invariant.

synthesis.rung-unit-second-free-number.formal:
\emph{Premise (rung unit), version 3.} Let $\mu > 0$ be the rung unit, the boost of the rung frame against the lift's null pair (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.rung-unit-transverse-to-norm}: $X_{\mu} = B_{1/\mu}\circ X_{1}$, rung component $c_{\mu} = -\mu w + \tfrac{\Lambda}{3\mu}v$; Theorem \claimref{synthesis.rung-unit-is-frame-boost}(4), where it is the boost $\lambda$), whose value in Hubble units is the ratio $v/H \approx 10^{44}$ of the weak scale to the Hubble scale (Proposition \claimref{standard-model.what-derived-what-not}(2)), with $H^{2} = \Lambda/3$. Transport does not fix the weak scale: every clause of totality holds in every frame and the rung component's tractor norm $-\Lambda/3$ is the same for every $\mu$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.rung-unit-transverse-to-norm}(1)), a change of $\mu$ is transverse to the norm mode through which $N = M_{P}/H$ enters (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.rung-unit-transverse-to-norm}(2)), and the only frame the structure singles out, the balanced frame $\mu = H$ in which the rung component lies in a $U(2)$-invariant real form up to a phase (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.rung-unit-is-frame-boost}(5), Theorem \claimref{synthesis.rung-unit-transverse-to-norm}(3)), would put the rung unit at the Hubble scale, about $44$ orders of magnitude below the observed weak scale. In the hypercharge D-term action of the rung field the frame is set by the level $\xi$, through the vacuum moment map $m^{*} = g'^{2}\xi/(g^{2} + g'^{2})$, a constant of the action that nothing in the stored structure fixes (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.rung-field-d-term-action-and-tree-masses-on-the-lift-locus}(2)). The premise: the level $\xi$, strictly the dimensionless $\xi G$ with $G = M_{P}^{-2}$, is a constant of the laws in the sense of clause (P) of Definition \claimref{obj.dark-energy.four-ways-number-enters-theory-shape}, fixed before variation and constant on each solution space, not derived and not a function of $N$: a second free number beside $N$. Consequently, under the Henneaux-Teitelboim promotion, which the series adopts (\claimref{programme.n-parameter-of-the-laws}) and under which $N$ is initial data:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $v/M_{P}$ is the law: it is the same on every solution up to a relative change of order $(H/v)^{4}$, since on the boundary of the orbit space the D-term potential does not contain $H$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.constant-d-term-level-fixes-weak-scale-against-planck-under-promotion}(1), (2));
\item[(b)] $v/H = (v/M_{P})N$ is initial data, the branch of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.einstein-route-makes-n-initial-data}(iii) in which $v/M_{P}$ is held;
\item[(c)] $Gv^{2}N^{2} = (v/H)^{2}$ is an identity, $(v/H)^{2}$ times $G\Lambda = 3N^{-2}$, not a law: its left side is a constant of the laws exactly when $v/H$ is (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.constant-d-term-level-fixes-weak-scale-against-planck-under-promotion}(3)).
\end{enumerate}
Under the action as written, where $N$ is a parameter of the laws, $v/M_{P}$ and $v/H$ are both constant on each solution space.

\emph{Note (the Hubble unit).} Here $H$ is the de Sitter rate of $\Lambda$, $H^{2} = \Lambda/3$, that is $H_{0}\sqrt{\Omega_{\Lambda}}$, the $H$ of $N = M_{P}/H$; some texts quote $v/H$ with $H_{0}$. The two differ by the factor $\sqrt{\Omega_{\Lambda}}$, of order one: $v/H \approx 2.1 \times 10^{44}$ with $H^{2} = \Lambda/3$ and $v/H_{0} \approx 1.7 \times 10^{44}$, so $\ln(v/H)$ changes by about $0.2$. The orders of magnitude quoted here hold for both.

\emph{Note (independence at fixed $N$).} Theorem \claimref{synthesis.d-term-level-free-at-fixed-n} gives two models of the premise set and the stored relations at their pinned versions with equal $G$, $\Lambda$ (so equal $N$) and equal couplings but different $\xi G$ and $v/M_{P}$, on the branch $W = \kappa\mathsf{N}^{2}$, which the record excludes: no stored relation fixes $\xi G$, and it is not a function of $N$. This is consistent with the premise and does not change it.

\emph{Version note.} Version 2 of this premise held $v/H$ as the parameter of the laws and $v/M_{P}$ as initial data under the promotion, with refutation condition a derivation of $v/H$ or a dynamics of the rung component under which $v/H$ would be initial data. The stored D-term action with its level a constant of the action, read under the promotion, is such a dynamics (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.constant-d-term-level-fixes-weak-scale-against-planck-under-promotion}): it met that condition, and the premise is restated here with the level as the constant of the laws.

\emph{Refutation condition.} The premise is false if the weak scale is derived, that is if $\xi G$ (equivalently $v/M_{P}$ up to relative order $(H/v)^{4}$) is computed from the transport structure, from another premise of the register, or from a dynamics with no further free dimensionless constant (for example a breaking pattern of the Pati-Salam stage that fixes the level, or a transmutation scale fixed by the couplings alone); or if it is shown to be a function of $N$; or if the rung action is coupled to the promoted $\Lambda$ so that the level is proportional to $\Lambda$ (a level $\hat{\xi}\Lambda$), under which $v/H$, not $v/M_{P}$, would be the law.

synthesis.slot-count-not-from-constituen.formal:
Let $\CS = \Lambda^{\mathrm{even}}W$ be the conformal spinor space as a module of $\mathrm{Spin}(6) \cong SL(4)$ (complexified), $\CSm = \Lambda^{\mathrm{odd}}W$ its dual, and let $U$ be $\CS$, $\CSm$ or $\CS \oplus \CSm$. For $K \ge 1$ let $F$ be a Fock-type space over the one-particle space $\C^{K} \otimes U$ (slot label times internal twistor label): a representation of $SL(K) \times SL(4)$ spanned by vectors $a^{\dagger}(u_{1})\cdots a^{\dagger}(u_{n})\Omega$, $u_{r} \in \C^{K} \otimes U$, with $\Omega$ invariant and $a^{\dagger}$ linear and equivariant (the exterior and symmetric algebras, and parastatistics Fock spaces, are examples). Let $\mathcal{S} = \Lambda^{\bullet}(\C^{2} \oplus W)$ be the state space of Hypothesis (S), with $SL(2)$ acting on the slot modes and $SL(4)$ on $\Lambda^{\bullet}W$, and $\mathcal{S}_{j} = \Lambda^{j}\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{\bullet}W$ its slot-number sectors.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(Even slot number.)} If $K$ is even, $F$ has no nonzero $SL(K)$-invariant vector in an $SL(4)$-isotypic component of type $\CS$ or $\CSm$.
\item \emph{(The (S) sectors that cannot be matched.)} In $\mathcal{S}$ the sectors $\mathcal{S}_{0}$ and $\mathcal{S}_{2}$ are $SL(2)$-invariant and each is $\CS \oplus \CSm$ under $SL(4)$; they hold the singlet species $\nu^{c}, u^{c}$ ($j = 0$) and $e^{c}, d^{c}$ ($j = 2$) of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.under-s-composites-state-space-spinor} and their conjugates. Hence for $K = 2$ every $SL(2) \times SL(4)$-equivariant linear map $\mathcal{S} \to F$ vanishes on $\mathcal{S}_{0} \oplus \mathcal{S}_{2}$, every equivariant map $F \to \mathcal{S}$ has image in $\mathcal{S}_{1}$, and $\mathcal{S}$ is not an $SL(2) \times SL(4)$-equivariant subquotient of any such $F$, whatever the statistics. Equivariance for the twistor group $SL(4)$ is needed: under the gauge group $U(2) \times U(3)$ alone the obstruction disappears, the central element $-1 \in SL(4)$ not being a gauge transformation.
\item \emph{(Odd $K$ is different.)} For $K = 3$ and $U = \CS$: the exterior algebra has no $SL(3)$-singlet copy of $\CS$ or $\CSm$ (computed, all degrees), while the symmetric algebra contains exactly one copy of $\det \otimes \CSm$ in degree three and none of $\CS$ in degree nine (computed). For $K = 4$ there is none in either algebra.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.slot-parity-decides-hypothesis.formal:
Let the composite's three pair modes $b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}$ be the fermionic ladder pairs of Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-pair-systems-two-chirality-halves}, odd elements of the tower's canonical anticommutation algebra, and let its two slot modes $s_{1}, s_{2}$ be elements of the same algebra such that: (a) each is homogeneous for the tower's grading, of parity $\pi \in \{0, 1\}$ (one for odd); (b) they are two-level, $s_{i}^{2} = 0$ and $s_{i}s_{i}^{*} + s_{i}^{*}s_{i} = 1$; (c) $U(2)$ acts on their span, $s_{i} \mapsto \sum_{l} A_{li}s_{l}$, preserving (b); (d) each slot operator commutes or anticommutes with each pair operator, and slot and pair annihilators share a vacuum $\lvert 0 \rangle$. Read the composite's chirality as the tower's grading, as Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-pair-systems-two-chirality-halves} reads it on $\Lambda^{\bullet}W$. Write $(j, k)$ for the component with $j$ slot and $k$ pair modes occupied. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the slot modes satisfy the canonical anticommutation relations among themselves; two-level slots that commute with each other admit no such $U(2)$ action (a rotated slot squares to a nonzero multiple of $s_{1}s_{2}$, and the doubly occupied state is multiplied by the permanent of $A$, not its determinant);
\item[(ii)] for either parity the states $s_{J}^{*}b_{K}^{*}\lvert 0 \rangle$ are thirty-two independent vectors on which $U(2) \times U(3)$ acts as $\Lambda^{\bullet}A \otimes \Lambda^{\bullet}B \cong \Lambda^{\bullet}(A \oplus B)$ on $\Lambda^{\bullet}(\C^{2} \oplus W)$: the state space and group action of Hypothesis (S);
\item[(iii)] the tower parity of the component $(j, k)$ is $(-1)^{\pi j + k}$, whichever of the two mutual signs in (d) holds; it is the parity of the total mode number, the chirality of Hypothesis (S), if and only if $\pi = 1$;
\item[(iv)] for $\pi = 0$ the two chirality halves are $\bar{Q} \oplus \bar{L} \oplus u^{c} \oplus d^{c} \oplus e^{c} \oplus \nu^{c}$, the components $(1,2), (1,0), (0,2), (2,2), (2,0), (0,0)$, and its conjugate. For the hypercharge $Y = (3j - 2k)/6$ the $[SU(3)]^{2}U(1)$ sum of Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-anomaly-coefficients} is $\mp\tfrac23$ and the $U(1)^{3}$ sum $\pm\tfrac49$, the other two vanishing; an abelian generator $\alpha j + \beta k + \gamma$ satisfies the three linear conditions if and only if $\beta = 0$ and $\gamma = -\alpha$, so none that tells a quark from a lepton is anomaly-free. For $\pi = 1$ the halves are the derived generation and its conjugate, and $Y$ passes all four conditions.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently: (v) the model with $\pi = 0$ has no pair with $[a, a^{*}] = 1$ (it lies in a finite floor, Proposition \claimref{field-theory.no-canonical-commutation-relations-tower}) and its slots are tower elements, yet the chirality clause of Hypothesis (S) fails in it, so (S) does not follow from the statistics no-go together with the requirement that the slots be tower elements; (vi) under (a) to (d), the tower grading as chirality, and the imported anomaly conditions for the hypercharge, the slots are odd and every clause of Hypothesis (S) holds, the mutual slot-pair sign being invisible to the state space, the group action and the grading.

synthesis.spin10-clause-content-is-the-n.formal:
Let $V = \Lambda^{\bullet}(\C^{2} \oplus W) = \Lambda^{\bullet}\C^{5}$ be the five-mode module of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hyp-s-slot-statistics}(ii), modes $1, 2$ the slots and $3, 4, 5$ the pairs, $N$ the total mode number, with the gauge algebra $\mathfrak{s}(\uu(2) \oplus \uu(3))$ acting by the bilinears of each block and $Y = (3n - 2k)/6$; let $\uu(5)$ be the span of all number-conserving bilinears $a^{\dagger}_{i}a_{j}$ and $\so(10)$ that of all bilinears, including $a_{i}a_{j}$ and $a^{\dagger}_{i}a^{\dagger}_{j}$, generating the $\Spin(10)$ of clause (v) there (Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-fock-representation-clifford-algebra-bogoliubov}); let $\gamma_{1}, \dots, \gamma_{10}$ be the Majorana generators. A CPT halving is as in Theorem \claimref{synthesis.cpt-closure-forces-slot-module-and-chirality}: an involution $\Gamma$ commuting with the gauge algebra whose $-1$ eigenspace is isomorphic to the conjugate of its $+1$ eigenspace. The neutral block is the span of the empty state $\Omega$ and the full state $\Omega^{\top} = a^{\dagger}_{1}\cdots a^{\dagger}_{5}\Omega$, the two copies of $(1,1)_{0}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Commutants.)} The commutant of the gauge algebra on $V$ has dimension $14$ ($10$ multiplicity-one components and the full $2 \times 2$ algebra of the neutral block; the charged weight spaces are one-dimensional), that of $\uu(5)$ dimension $6$ (one projector per degree), that of $\so(10)$ dimension $2$ (the two parity projectors), and that of the $\gamma_{p}$ dimension $1$; $\gamma_{1}\cdots\gamma_{10} = -(-1)^{N}$.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(The ladder of halvings.)} The CPT halvings commuting with the gauge algebra are: a sign on each of the five conjugate pairs of charged components ($32$ patterns), together with any pair of complementary lines in the neutral block, one in each half. Those commuting also with $\uu(5)$ have the signs constant on each degree and the neutral lines equal to $\C\Omega$ and $\C\Omega^{\top}$ in either order: $8$ halvings (commuting with the total mode number $N$ alone already forces the neutral lines to be $\C\Omega$ and $\C\Omega^{\top}$, and after anomaly freedom gives the same four below; only the intermediate count $8$ needs $\uu(5)$). With the chirality of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.cpt-closure-forces-slot-module-and-chirality}(4) on the charged components (anomaly freedom), $4$ remain: the overall label, and whether $\Omega$ or $\Omega^{\top}$ lies in the particle half. The two choices are unitarily equivalent: the swap $S$ of $\Omega$ and $\Omega^{\top}$, the identity elsewhere, is an involution commuting with the gauge algebra, and the vacuum-swapped halving is $S(-1)^{N}S$; so no gauge quantum number sees the difference, and only the $\so(10)$ pair operators, which $S$ does not commute with, do. Those commuting with $\so(10)$ are exactly $\pm(-1)^{N}$: the vacuum-swapped halving (even charged components with $\Omega^{\top}$ against odd ones with $\Omega$) commutes with $\uu(5)$, is anomaly-free, and fails on the creation bracket $a^{\dagger}_{1}a^{\dagger}_{2}$.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(The clause as a grading.)} $\Gamma = \pm(-1)^{N}$ are exactly the involutions of $V$ for which every $\gamma_{p}$ is odd, $\Gamma\gamma_{p}\Gamma = -\gamma_{p}$, that is, the gradings of the joint Clifford system; for the vacuum-swapped halving some $\gamma_{p}$ is not odd. Hence the $\Spin(10)$ form of the chirality clause of the slot-module premise (its version 2) is equivalent to the grading form (its version 3): the chirality is the grading of the joint Clifford system of clause (f); and the clause's content beyond the gauge symmetry, number conservation and anomaly freedom is the single choice of $\Omega$ rather than $\Omega^{\top}$ as the particle half's neutral singlet. It is not derivable from those: the vacuum-swapped halving satisfies all of them. (The current version of the premise states the clause as commuting with the Pati-Salam algebra of the chain, which for an anomaly-free halving admits the same two halvings $\pm(-1)^{N}$.)
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.up-down-split-under-conjugate-.formal:
Under Hypothesis (S), on the module of Proposition \claimref{synthesis.georgi-jarlskog-majorana-moment-map}, let $c = a\,v_{4} + b\,w_{4}$ be the rung vector of Corollary \claimref{standard-model.one-vev-vector-relations-breaking-pattern}, with down-type and charged-lepton masses read from $c$ and up-type and Dirac-neutrino masses through $c^{*} = \bar{b}\,v_{4} + \bar{a}\,w_{4}$. For a diagonal weighting $g$ read the same way (down-type and charged-lepton entries from $b\,w_{4}g$, up-type and Dirac-neutrino entries from $\bar{b}\,v_{4}g$), with mass forms $B(\psi, X\varphi)$ on the sixteen (Lemma \claimref{standard-model.invariant-pairing-five-modes}), the sixteen being the particle half $\pm(-1)^{N}$ of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.hyp-s-slot-statistics}, with $\nu^{c}$ the empty state (the halving the Pati-Salam stage of the chain forces, Theorem \claimref{synthesis.neutral-singlet-bit-decided-by-gauge-algebra-leaving-u5}(iii); the Dirac-neutrino statements need that halving), write the symmetric entries relative to those of the $10$ forms of $w_{4}$ and $v_{4}$ on the same species pairs (Theorem \claimref{standard-model.yukawa-pattern-two-rung-module}). Let $T$, $\mu_{P}$, $\mu_{R}$, $\chi$, $X_{4} = 8\mu_{R}\mu_{P}$ and the degree-four part $G_{4}$ of $TT^{\dagger}$ be as in Propositions \claimref{standard-model.majorana-element}, \claimref{synthesis.georgi-jarlskog-majorana-moment-map} and \claimref{synthesis.georgi-jarlskog-square-of-majorana-element}, and let $P_{4}$ and $R_{4}$ be the pair-only and rung-only parts of $G_{4}$, so that $G_{4} = P_{4} + X_{4} + R_{4}$ with $P_{4} = \tfrac{3}{32}(B - L)(-1)^{N_{P}}$ and $R_{4} = -\tfrac{1}{32}\chi$ (derived in the proof). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] for $g = h(T_{3R}, B - L)$ the down-type entries are $e + o$ and the up-type entries $e - o$ on each sector, $e$ and $o$ the contributions of the parts of $h$ even and odd in $T_{3R}$; so a $T_{3R}$-even weighting gives $m_{u} = m_{d}$ and $m_{\nu}^{D} = m_{e}$, and in particular the $10$ and every $f(B - L)$, among them $P_{4}$, never split up-type from down-type entries;
\item[(ii)] relative to the $10$ (lepton, quark): $P_{4}$ gives $(\tfrac{3}{32}, -\tfrac{1}{32})$ in both sectors; $X_{4}$ gives $(-\tfrac{3}{32}, \tfrac{1}{32})$ down and $(\tfrac{3}{32}, -\tfrac{1}{32})$ up; $\mu_{R} = \tfrac14 T_{3R}$ gives $(\tfrac{1}{16}, \tfrac{1}{16})$ down and $(-\tfrac{1}{16}, -\tfrac{1}{16})$ up; $R_{4}$ and $\mu_{P}$ give no symmetric entry;
\item[(iii)] hence $G_{4}$ gives no down-type or charged-lepton entry and up-type entries equal to $-\tfrac{3}{16}$ times the up-type Georgi-Jarlskog form $v_{4}(B - L)\chi$ of Proposition \claimref{families.doublet-direction-module-georgi-jarlskog-clebsch}(iv), so $|m_{\nu}^{D}| = 3|m_{u}|$ for this contribution; and the square through degree four, $\tfrac{1}{32} + \tfrac12\mu + G_{4}$, gives the down sector the $10$ relation ($m_{e} = m_{d}$) with weight $\tfrac{1}{16}$ and the up sector that $126$-type form alone; the whole square $TT^{\dagger}$ gives no entry.
\end{enumerate}
So under the conjugate reading the up-down split of a rung composite is carried by its $\su(2)_{R}$-odd part, and the degree-four part of the square of the Majorana element, whose orientation in $\su(2)_{R}$ is that of $T$, supplies one that acts on the up sector alone; it is a truncation that no stored dynamics selects, and the whole square gives no entry on the sixteen.

\emph{Note (the covariant completion, 2026-11-01).} The conjugate reading stands for the $\su(2)_{R}$-covariant completion of the coupling: one family matrix $Y$ on $c$ and one $\tilde{Y}$ on $c^{*}$, each on the whole vector, $M_{d} = bY + \bar{a}\tilde{Y}$, $M_{u} = aY + \bar{b}\tilde{Y}$ (\claimref{synthesis.rung-field-coupling-to-its-conjugate-is-the-second-family-matrix}). The per-sector reading used here (down-type entries from $c$ or $c\,g$, up-type entries from $c^{*}$ or its weighting, one family matrix) is its leading mass reading, equal to it up to relative corrections $e^{-2\eta}$ on the lift family, and is not $\su(2)_{R}$ covariant: it vanishes on the $a$ half of $c$, which the right-handed $\su(2)$ carries into the $b$ half. The clauses above are statements about that leading reading; under the completion an independent $\tilde{Y}$ splits up from down already for one rung vector, by $\tr H_{u} - \tr H_{d} = 2m(c)(\|Y\|^{2} - \|\tilde{Y}\|^{2})$ (loc. cit.), the $T_{3R}$-odd channel of (i) in another guise (\claimref{synthesis.conjugate-reading-up-down-split-is-t3r-odd-or-cp-odd}).
